{"timestamp":"2026-10-16T17:31:55.619Z","type":"tool","step":"PLAN","details":{"toolName":"edit","args":{"newString":"## Decisions (user, 2026-10-16 — resolves the assumptions below)\n\n1. **State indicator**: nameplate rendering. PLUS a config-gated DEBUG mode writing an\n   agent activity log to disk (`debug/siasworkshop/…`, like the inspector snapshots)\n   so the coordinator can read state/decisions from the repo without being in-game.\n2. **Autonomy — the north star (long-term, explicitly NOT v2 scope)**: progressively\n   move the decision framework to the LLM and keep execution in the mod, iteration by\n   iteration — no rush. LLM works at the highest abstraction; the mod queues and runs\n   large action sequences from well-defined LLM plans (example given: \"farm 1k of each\n   crop sustainably using several agents, report when done\" → LLM emits a sequential\n   JSON plan → mod executes over a long horizon). Furthest horizon: the model may\n   progressively redefine how actions are implemented (usage-derived learning feeds\n   back into the mod's capabilities). Each release moves some decisions up and some\n   actions down; that is the roadmap philosophy.\n3. **Task board stays** — also as the inter-agent collaboration substrate beneath\n   pattern workflows.\n4. **Sidecar stack: Python is fine, gated by a feature flag** (default OFF). The MCP\n   path must be opt-in via config so CurseForge installs never require Python until a\n   polished release exists. Dev builds run the full toolset.","oldString":"## What was NOT said (assumptions to validate)\n\n- Whether state-indicator text should be nameplate-only, chat-only, or both.\n- How much per-turn autonomy: one tool call per decision cycle vs. an entire\n  pattern walk inside one session.\n- Whether the existing task board remains the planning substrate beneath\n  patterns, or gets subsumed by pattern workflows.","path":"mdocs/wiki/setup/agent-ux-feedback-2026-10-16.md"}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-16T17:31:55.616Z","type":"tool","step":"PLAN","details":{"toolName":"edit","args":{"newString":"## Decisions (user, 2026-10-16 — resolves the assumptions below)\n\n1. **State indicator**: nameplate rendering. PLUS a config-gated DEBUG mode writing an\n   agent activity log to disk (`debug/siasworkshop/…`, like the inspector snapshots)\n   so the coordinator can read state/decisions from the repo without being in-game.\n2. **Autonomy — the north star (long-term, explicitly NOT v2 scope)**: progressively\n   move the decision framework to the LLM and keep execution in the mod, iteration by\n   iteration — no rush. LLM works at the highest abstraction; the mod queues and runs\n   large action sequences from well-defined LLM plans (example given: \"farm 1k of each\n   crop sustainably using several agents, report when done\" → LLM emits a sequential\n   JSON plan → mod executes over a long horizon). Furthest horizon: the model may\n   progressively redefine how actions are implemented (usage-derived learning feeds\n   back into the mod's capabilities). Each release moves some decisions up and some\n   actions down; that is the roadmap philosophy.\n3. **Task board stays** — also as the inter-agent collaboration substrate beneath\n   pattern workflows.\n4. **Sidecar stack: Python is fine, gated by a feature flag** (default OFF). The MCP\n   path must be opt-in via config so CurseForge installs never require Python until a\n   polished release exists. Dev builds run the full toolset.","oldString":"## What was NOT said (assumptions to validate)\n\n- Whether state-indicator text should be nameplate-only, chat-only, or both.\n- How much per-turn autonomy: one tool call per decision cycle vs. an entire\n  pattern walk inside one session.\n- Whether the existing task board remains the planning substrate beneath\n  patterns, or gets subsumed by pattern workflows.","path":"mdocs/wiki/setup/agent-ux-feedback-2026-10-16.md"}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-16T17:32:14.382Z","type":"tool","step":"PLAN","details":{"toolName":"edit","args":{"newString":"<!-- Agents: append new entries below this line, in SEQ order. -->\n\n`SEQ-016 | 2026-10-16 | coordinator | DECISION | Four user decisions recorded (wiki: setup/agent-ux-feedback-2026-10-16 + initiative): (1) state indicator = nameplate + config-gated DEBUG activity log on disk for coordinator visibility; (2) AUTONOMY NORTH STAR — progressively move decisions to the LLM (highest abstraction, sequential JSON plans the mod queues/executes over long horizons, multi-agent) and actions to the mod; furthest horizon = model progressively redefines action implementations from usage learning (long-term research only); iterate per release, no rush; (3) task board stays, incl. as inter-agent collaboration substrate; (4) Python sidecar approved WITH feature flag default-OFF (no Python dependency for CurseForge installs; dev runs full toolset). Next implementation: agent state visibility (nameplate states, working say(), debug activity log) as its own initiative, then MCP spike.`","oldString":"<!-- Agents: append new entries below this line, in SEQ order. -->","path":"mdocs/wiki/setup/agent-shared-ledger.md"}}}
