rees, not mapped verbs** (user feedback\n  2026-10-16, `setup/agent-ux-feedback-2026-10-16`): the catalog is leveled and\n  tree-structured — category `building` holds patterns like \"think through a build\n  request\" → \"analyze terrain / find where\" → \"design a build\" → \"select materials\"\n  → \"create blueprint\" → \"apply blueprint\". The world is a **state machine**:\n  observe state via MCP, request state changes via patterns, observe convergence.\n  LLM intelligence plans at a high level; the mod executes the leaf effects (place,\n  build, farm, mine). MCP sessions are **multi-step workflows**, not one-shot\n  decision calls.\n- **North star (user, 2026-10-16): progressive transfer of decisions to the LLM and\n  actions to the mod.** Each iteration moves some decisions up (higher abstraction,\n  bigger plans) and some execution down (into mod code). Concrete mechanism en route:\n  the LLM issues well-defined plans (sequential JSON); the mod queues and executes\n  them over long horizons (multi-agent, report-on-completion). Furthest horizon — the\n  model progressively redefines how actions are implemented from usage-derived\n  learning — is explicitly long-term research, not committed scope.\n- **MCP path is feature-flagged, default OFF** (user, 2026-10-16): CurseForge installs\n  must not require Python; the sidecar client activates only via config opt-in. Dev\n  builds run the full toolset.\n\n## Motivation (pain observed in the live test, SEQ-011)\n\nEvery model behaves differently in the same pipeline, and v1 absorbed the variance\nad hoc inside `LlmClient`:\n\n- Reasoning models burn 500–900 hidden tokens before answering (empty `content` at\n  512 `max_tokens`); non-reasoning models answer in ~20 tokens. Budget is per-model\n  tuning, not contract.\n- Output hygiene varies: markdown code fences despite \"JSON only\", prose wrappers,\n  occasionally unparseable bodies (`bad_json`).\n- Wire compat varies: llama.cpp rejects `response_format: json_object`; OpenAI\n  accepts it; some servers support `json_schema` constrained decoding, some don't.\n- Prompt compliance varies by family (Qwen vs Llama vs GPT): currently one fixed\n  `LlmPrompts` system line, one-size-fits-all.\n\n## Research questions (given the direction above)\n\n1. Which local sizes (4B/14B/27B on M3 Pro) and cloud models support tool/function\n   calling reliably enough for a growing tool catalog? Failure modes when\n   unsupported — and does prompt-constrained JSON remain the fallback inside the\n   sidecar for those?\n2. Does tool-calling measurably reduce malformed/unusable decisions vs the current\n   prompt-constrained JSON path? (Metric target: `bad_json`/fallback rate.)\n3. **Pattern-tree design**: descriptor format for categories → patterns →\n   sub-tools/effects; how sub-tool discovery works across turns; how prompts stay\n   small when the catalog grows (retrieve-and-inject per situation, like build\n   cards today); how the multi-step session state is held (in the sidecar? in the\n   mod against the task board?).\n4. What does \"stable behavior\" mean measurably — a scenario corpus (recorded\n   observations + expected action classes) replayable offline against any endpoint;\n   can the sidecar host this model-behavior CI (A/B profiles, regression runs)?\n5. Ops: sidecar lifecycle (dev loop: process manager? Gradle task?), extra-hop\n   latency budget, offline-first preserved (template fallback when sidecar/endpoint\n   down — the current in-mod template path stays as the last resort).\n\n## Considered and set aside (rationale recorded)\n\n- **OpenAI-shim/proxy normalization (LiteLLM-style)**: rejected — user wants\n  normalization in the MCP sidecar; a shim would still leave tool semantics\n  model-specific.\n- **Per-model profiles inside the mod**: demoted — profiles move into the sidecar;\n  the mod stays model-agnostic.\n- **Low-level mapped tools** (\"open chest\", \"place block T at X,Y\"): rejected by\n  user (2026-10-16) — the catalog exposes composable patterns; leaf effects are\n  executed by the mod, not driven step-by-step by the model.\n- **Status quo (in-mod normalization)**: the v1 fallback path stays for offline, but\n  is not the integration strategy.\n\n## Plan\n\n- [x] Finish the cloud-model test round (SEQ-014: trailing-slash 404, seconds-fast\n      decisions, shelter ping-pong) — requirements input collected\n- [x] Collect + prioritize the user's playtest feedback batch (SEQ-015)\n- [x] Prerequisite feature (separate initiative): agent state visibility — nameplate\n      states + working say() + config-gated DEBUG activity log (DONE, f1e1c71)\n- [x] Design the pattern-tree descriptor format (category → patterns → effects),\n      building-workflow example as the reference walk\n      → `reference/mcp-pattern-tree-design` (2026-10-16)\n- [x] Spike: minimal sidecar exposing the 3 decision tools to one local model and\n      one cloud model; measure decision latency + malformed-decision rate vs v1\n      → S0 done under `sidecar/` (2026-10-16, results SEQ-020): replay 20/20,\n      cloud 9/9 ok, local v1 failed while both MCP modes passed\n- [ ] Feature-flag wiring: MCP client path default-OFF, opt-in via config (no Python\n      dependency for CurseForge installs) — S1, with in-mod gametest\n- [ ] S2: first real pattern tree against the building walk (three patterns against\n      real mod machinery, same scenario as a v1 build task)\n- [ ] Scenario corpus + assertions for question 4\n- [ ] Recommendation → `reference/` wiki; if adopted, plan the LlmClient→MCP-client\n      migration (config: sidecar address instead of endpoint/model/apiKey)\n\n## Progress Log\n\n- [2026-10-15] Initiative created from user note after LLM live-test session\n      (commits f974a47, c9ebf8d, 212f147, 2daa823; ledger SEQ-011).\n- [2026-10-15] User direction recorded: MCP-sidecar normalization (not shim), the\n      mod exposes its action/pattern catalog as MCP tools — the tool catalog is the\n      stable model↔mod interface, growing with mod capabilities; per-model\n      optimization profiles allowed for hot models. Priority low→medium.\n- [2026-10-16] Cloud test round done (SEQ-014) — requirements input collected.\n- [2026-10-16] User feedback batch (wiki: setup/agent-ux-feedback-2026-10-16):\n      pattern-tree tool catalog (world = observable state machine, LLM plans, mod\n      executes leaf effects); in-game agent state visibility is the agreed\n      pre-sidecar feature. Research questions refocused; low-level mapped tools\n      recorded as rejected.\n- [2026-10-16] Resumed per handoff session-handoff-2026-10-16. Pattern-tree\n      descriptor design written: wiki reference/mcp-pattern-tree-design (roles+stdio\n      transport, 4 tool kinds, descriptor format, pattern-card expansion,\n      session-state split, safety, flag, prompt economy, building-walk reference\n      trace, S0-S2 spike plan, open questions).\n- [2026-10-16] S0 spike done under `sidecar/` (stdlib-only Python: JSON-RPC/MCP\n      client, mock mod in the server role, 3-mode A/B runner, asserted replay\n      self-test 20/20). Cloud (deepseek-pro, n=3/mode): 9/9 ok, 0 malformed —\n      v1 1.1s / mcp-prompt 1.2s / mcp-native 1.4s, 21/48/68 completion tokens.\n      Local (bonsai-27b, n=1/mode): v1 FAILED (120s timeout; previous run\n      empty_content:length at 1024 — 2/2 budget burnouts on the v1 prompt) while\n      BOTH MCP modes succeeded (~39s, ~350-390 tokens). LM Studio accepted native\n      tool-calling for bonsai. Details + findings: ledger SEQ-020; sidecar/README.md;\n      metrics in sidecar/metrics-*.jsonl.\n","path":"mdocs/initiatives/mcp-model-behavior-research-2026-10-15--2026-10-15.md"}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-16T21:40:38.626Z","type":"tool","initiativeId":"mcp-model-behavior-research--2026-10-15","step":"EXECUTE","details":{"toolName":"edit","args":{"newString":"`SEQ-020 | 2026-10-16 | coordinator | RESOLVED | S0 spike DONE (sidecar/, stdlib-only Python, uncommitted). Built: JSON-RPC/MCP client over pipes (D2 wire, mock mod in the server role), schema-subset validator (D7 boundary), native+prompt catalog renderers, per-model profiles, 3-mode A/B runner (v1 / mcp-prompt / mcp-native), asserted replay self-test 20/20 (the gametest analog: clean/fenced/prose/unknown-tool/bad-args/exec-reject/native-call/garbage cases). RESULTS — cloud ~deepseek/deepseek-pro-latest (n=3/mode): 9/9 valid, 0 malformed; latency v1 1.1s / mcp-prompt 1.2s / mcp-native 1.4s; completion tokens 21/48/68 — MCP surfaces cost +0.1-0.3s and +27-47 tok, negligible. Local prism-ml/bonsai-27b (n=1/mode): v1 prompt FAILED — 120s timeout (previous run: empty_content:length at maxTokens 1024; 2/2 budget burnouts) — while BOTH MCP modes succeeded (mcp-prompt 39.6s/390 tok, mcp-native 38.5s/341 tok, both exact-key claims). The structured tool contract contains a reasoning model's deliberation where the v1 freeform decision prompt does not (small n, consistent mechanism). FINDINGS: (1) wire names must be MCP-legal — tool-name schema ^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]{1,64}$ forbids dots; dotted ids stay internal, observe.self→observe_self at the protocol edge (design doc amended); (2) native-mode models call WHATEVER tools they're given — with observe.* offered, deepseek re-observed 2/3 runs instead of deciding (multi-step instinct, good for S2; S0 restricts the native leg to the decision effect set for parity); (3) LM Studio (llama.cpp) accepted native tool-calling for bonsai-27b — local-native profiles viable, revisit bonsai profile default; (4) one client bug found+fixed (tool_calls accumulation on first native call). Bug worth keeping: the mdocs initiative.update CLI CLOBBERS the initiative body (Plan/Direction sections) — restored by hand from session context; prefer direct file edits + validate for initiative content going forward. Next: S1 (in-mod behind flag + gametest) or S2 (first real pattern tree) — user picks.`\n\n`SEQ-011 | 2026-10-15 | coordinator | RESOLVED | Live smoke tests 1-3 ALL PASSED","oldString":"`SEQ-011 | 2026-10-15 | coordinator | RESOLVED | Live smoke tests 1-3 ALL PASSED","path":"mdocs/wiki/setup/agent-shared-ledger.md"}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-16T21:40:38.630Z","type":"tool","initiativeId":"mcp-model-behavior-research--2026-10-15","step":"EXECUTE","details":{"toolName":"edit","args":{"newString":"`SEQ-020 | 2026-10-16 | coordinator | RESOLVED | S0 spike DONE (sidecar/, stdlib-only Python, uncommitted). Built: JSON-RPC/MCP client over pipes (D2 wire, mock mod in the server role), schema-subset validator (D7 boundary), native+prompt catalog renderers, per-model profiles, 3-mode A/B runner (v1 / mcp-prompt / mcp-native), asserted replay self-test 20/20 (the gametest analog: clean/fenced/prose/unknown-tool/bad-args/exec-reject/native-call/garbage cases). RESULTS — cloud ~deepseek/deepseek-pro-latest (n=3/mode): 9/9 valid, 0 malformed; latency v1 1.1s / mcp-prompt 1.2s / mcp-native 1.4s; completion tokens 21/48/68 — MCP surfaces cost +0.1-0.3s and +27-47 tok, negligible. Local prism-ml/bonsai-27b (n=1/mode): v1 prompt FAILED — 120s timeout (previous run: empty_content:length at maxTokens 1024; 2/2 budget burnouts) — while BOTH MCP modes succeeded (mcp-prompt 39.6s/390 tok, mcp-native 38.5s/341 tok, both exact-key claims). The structured tool contract contains a reasoning model's deliberation where the v1 freeform decision prompt does not (small n, consistent mechanism). FINDINGS: (1) wire names must be MCP-legal — tool-name schema ^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]{1,64}$ forbids dots; dotted ids stay internal, observe.self→observe_self at the protocol edge (design doc amended); (2) native-mode models call WHATEVER tools they're given — with observe.* offered, deepseek re-observed 2/3 runs instead of deciding (multi-step instinct, good for S2; S0 restricts the native leg to the decision effect set for parity); (3) LM Studio (llama.cpp) accepted native tool-calling for bonsai-27b — local-native profiles viable, revisit bonsai profile default; (4) one client bug found+fixed (tool_calls accumulation on first native call). Bug worth keeping: the mdocs initiative.update CLI CLOBBERS the initiative body (Plan/Direction sections) — restored by hand from session context; prefer direct file edits + validate for initiative content going forward. Next: S1 (in-mod behind flag + gametest) or S2 (first real pattern tree) — user picks.`\n\n`SEQ-011 | 2026-10-15 | coordinator | RESOLVED | Live smoke tests 1-3 ALL PASSED","oldString":"`SEQ-011 | 2026-10-15 | coordinator | RESOLVED | Live smoke tests 1-3 ALL PASSED","path":"mdocs/wiki/setup/agent-shared-ledger.md"}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-16T21:40:47.702Z","type":"tool","initiativeId":"mcp-model-behavior-research--2026-10-15","step":"EXECUTE","details":{"toolName":"edit","args":{"newString":"**D4 — Descriptor format.** Catalog JSON, versioned, served by the mod\n(`GET` equivalent = one MCP resource/read or the root list). Tool ids are stable dot\npaths internally; **wire names replace dots with underscores** — the MCP tool-name\nschema is `^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]{1,64}$` and forbids dots (`observe.self` → `observe_self`;\nS0 finding). `inputs` is a JSON-Schema subset (the same subset `ArgShape` hand-rolls\ntoday, so the validator carries over). Effects declare `convergence` — how to observe\nthe change completing (board task status).","oldString":"**D4 — Descriptor format.** Catalog JSON, versioned, served by the mod\n(`GET` equivalent = one MCP resource/read or the root list). Tool ids are stable dot\npaths; `inputs` is a JSON-Schema subset (the same subset `ArgShape` hand-rolls today,\nso the validator carries over). Effects declare `convergence` — how to observe the\nchange completing (board task status).","path":"mdocs/wiki/reference/mcp-pattern-tree-design.md"}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-16T21:40:47.706Z","type":"tool","initiativeId":"mcp-model-behavior-research--2026-10-15","step":"EXECUTE","details":{"toolName":"edit","args":{"newString":"**D4 — Descriptor format.** Catalog JSON, versioned, served by the mod\n(`GET` equivalent = one MCP resource/read or the root list). Tool ids are stable dot\npaths internally; **wire names replace dots with underscores** — the MCP tool-name\nschema is `^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]{1,64}$` and forbids dots (`observe.self` → `observe_self`;\nS0 finding). `inputs` is a JSON-Schema subset (the same subset `ArgShape` hand-rolls\ntoday, so the validator carries over). Effects declare `convergence` — how to observe\nthe change completing (board task status).","oldString":"**D4 — Descriptor format.** Catalog JSON, versioned, served by the mod\n(`GET` equivalent = one MCP resource/read or the root list). Tool ids are stable dot\npaths; `inputs` is a JSON-Schema subset (the same subset `ArgShape` hand-rolls today,\nso the validator carries over). Effects declare `convergence` — how to observe the\nchange completing (board task status).","path":"mdocs/wiki/reference/mcp-pattern-tree-design.md"}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-16T21:40:58.791Z","type":"tool","initiativeId":"mcp-model-behavior-research--2026-10-15","step":"EXECUTE","details":{"toolName":"edit","args":{"newString":"## S0 spike results (2026-10-16)\n\nS0 ran the 3-tool parity surface (`claim`/`rest`/`say` + `observe.*`) against one\nlocal and one cloud model, A/B against the v1 prompt — see ledger SEQ-020 and\n`sidecar/` (replay self-test 20/20 asserted cases).\n\n- **Cloud (deepseek-pro, n=3/mode): 9/9 valid, 0 malformed.** Latency v1 1.1s /\n  mcp-prompt 1.2s / mcp-native 1.4s; completion tokens 21/48/68. MCP surfaces cost\n  +0.1–0.3s and +27–47 tokens per decision — parity at negligible cost.\n- **Local reasoning model (bonsai-27b, n=1/mode): v1 prompt failed twice**\n  (`empty_content:length` at 1024 tokens; then 120s timeout — the SEQ-011 budget\n  burnout) **while both MCP modes succeeded** (~39s, ~350–390 tokens, exact-key\n  claims). The structured tool contract contains reasoning-model deliberation where\n  the freeform decision prompt does not. Small n; the mechanism (constrained output\n  shortens deliberation) is consistent — re-measure with the scenario corpus (Q4).\n- **Native-mode models call whatever tools they are given.** With `observe.*`\n  offered alongside the decision set, deepseek re-observed instead of deciding 2/3\n  runs. Sensible multi-step instinct (supports D5/D6); S0 restricts the native leg to\n  the decision set for parity, and D5's situational card gating becomes a hard\n  requirement, not an optimization.\n- **LM Studio (llama.cpp) accepted native tool-calling for bonsai-27b** — local\n  native profiles are viable; the prompt-mode default stays until the corpus (Q4)\n  says otherwise.\n- The v1 baseline's local failure also reframes Q2: the win of tool-calling is not\n  just fewer malformed replies, it is **containing deliberation budget** on reasoning\n  models.\n\n## Spike plan (next, per initiative)","oldString":"## Spike plan (next, per initiative)","path":"mdocs/wiki/reference/mcp-pattern-tree-design.md"}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-16T21:40:58.795Z","type":"tool","initiativeId":"mcp-model-behavior-research--2026-10-15","step":"EXECUTE","details":{"toolName":"edit","args":{"newString":"## S0 spike results (2026-10-16)\n\nS0 ran the 3-tool parity surface (`claim`/`rest`/`say` + `observe.*`) against one\nlocal and one cloud model, A/B against the v1 prompt — see ledger SEQ-020 and\n`sidecar/` (replay self-test 20/20 asserted cases).\n\n- **Cloud (deepseek-pro, n=3/mode): 9/9 valid, 0 malformed.** Latency v1 1.1s /\n  mcp-prompt 1.2s / mcp-native 1.4s; completion tokens 21/48/68. MCP surfaces cost\n  +0.1–0.3s and +27–47 tokens per decision — parity at negligible cost.\n- **Local reasoning model (bonsai-27b, n=1/mode): v1 prompt failed twice**\n  (`empty_content:length` at 1024 tokens; then 120s timeout — the SEQ-011 budget\n  burnout) **while both MCP modes succeeded** (~39s, ~350–390 tokens, exact-key\n  claims). The structured tool contract contains reasoning-model deliberation where\n  the freeform decision prompt does not. Small n; the mechanism (constrained output\n  shortens deliberation) is consistent — re-measure with the scenario corpus (Q4).\n- **Native-mode models call whatever tools they are given.** With `observe.*`\n  offered alongside the decision set, deepseek re-observed instead of deciding 2/3\n  runs. Sensible multi-step instinct (supports D5/D6); S0 restricts the native leg to\n  the decision set for parity, and D5's situational card gating becomes a hard\n  requirement, not an optimization.\n- **LM Studio (llama.cpp) accepted native tool-calling for bonsai-27b** — local\n  native profiles are viable; the prompt-mode default stays until the corpus (Q4)\n  says otherwise.\n- The v1 baseline's local failure also reframes Q2: the win of tool-calling is not\n  just fewer malformed replies, it is **containing deliberation budget** on reasoning\n  models.\n\n## Spike plan (next, per initiative)","oldString":"## Spike plan (next, per initiative)","path":"mdocs/w